      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Photodynamic therapy (PDT) using topical application of aminolevulinic acid (ALA) is an effective treatment for acne vulgaris. However, there is no clear consensus on the treatment regime in Asians.<BR />"
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "To determine the efficacy, safety and tolerability of 5% ALA PDT in the treatment of truncal acne in Asians.<BR />"
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Patients with truncal acne were treated with 5%-ALA under occlusion for 3 hours. All were subsequently treated with a red light source at wavelength 630 nm and an irradiance of 38mW/cm<sup>2<\/sup> giving a total dose of 37 J/cm<sup>2<\/sup>. The numbers of acne lesions were recorded at baseline and regular intervals after treatment together with any adverse effects.<BR />"
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Fifteen patients were recruited. Overall, there was a 64.2% reduction in the inflammatory lesions count and a 24.3% reduction in the non-inflammatory lesions count at the end of the 12 weeks follow-up. Both mean lesions counts were significantly lower than baseline at all follow-up time points with paired t tests (all P values &lt;0.05). Pain was well tolerated among our patients.<BR />"
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "A single treatment session of 5%-ALA PDT was effective for the treatment of truncal acne with little side effects and acceptable in our Asian patients.<BR /><BR /> <em>J Drugs Dermatol<\/em>. 2016;15(6):727-732."
        }
      ]},
